# 2. Large Language Model (LLM) Research

## What is an LLM?

A Large Language Model is a neural language model capable of understanding and generating natural-language text. Modern LLM systems are strongly associated with the Transformer architecture.

The Transformer architecture was introduced by Vaswani et al. in **Attention Is All You Need (2017)**.

## Why use an LLM in this project?

A traditional dashboard requires users to select filters, charts or predefined reports. An LLM can provide a natural-language interface so HR users can ask questions conversationally.

### Example

HR user:

> Which department has the highest attrition?

The system can interpret the question, request the required analytical result and explain it in natural language.

Another example:

> What factors are associated with attrition among employees working overtime?

The AI layer can combine verified analytics and model outputs and present them in an HR-friendly response.

## Proposed LLM responsibilities

1. Understand natural-language workforce questions.
2. Determine which tools or information sources are required.
3. Summarize SQL results.
4. Explain machine-learning predictions.
5. Convert technical results into understandable business language.
6. Generate recommendations using verified evidence.

## Important design principle

The LLM should not invent workforce statistics. Numerical answers should come from the SQL or analytics tools. Organization-specific policy answers should be grounded in retrieved HR documents through RAG.

This design reduces unsupported or hallucinated answers.

## Reference

Vaswani, A. et al. (2017), *Attention Is All You Need*.
